{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are generally too high vs. realized prices (often by ~2–6 EUR), especially in downtrends.",
    "Trend/lag error: the model reacts too slowly to bearish momentum; it projects stabilization/sideways while the market continues drifting lower for ~1–2 weeks.",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: forecasted recoveries after dips are too strong and too early; realized rebounds are smaller and delayed.",
    "Horizon dependence: errors widen with lead time—first ~3–5 days are closer, while days ~7–20 show the largest overestimation during selloffs.",
    "Volatility miscalibration: the model underestimates drawdown depth (misses lower lows) and produces smoother paths than realized, implying insufficient downside tail risk."
  ]
}
